WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.549
An experience beyond limits... pain and pleasure&nbsp;indivisible. 

00:00:04.549 --> 00:00:06.902
This is the&nbsp;
untold truth of "Hellraiser."

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:11.840
What's in a name? Well, quite a lot when it&nbsp;
comes to a movie. Just imagine if "Toy Story"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.920
had been called "Toyz in the Hood," "Alien"&nbsp;
had been called "Star Beast," or "Back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:21.360
Future" had been called "Spaceman from Pluto" —&nbsp;
because they all almost were. Would these films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.400
still have gone on to be box-office smashes and&nbsp;
critical faves? We'll never know. But what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:32.080
do know is that "Hellraiser" was very nearly&nbsp;
called "Sadomasochists From Beyond the Grave."

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.400
Clive Barker explained in a 1987&nbsp;
interview with Fangoria that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.400
originally wanted "Hellraiser" to have&nbsp;
a very different title. He revealed —

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:44.000
"The imagery we're employing is,&nbsp;
as far as the creatures from hell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.160
are concerned, something that hasn't been&nbsp;
done before. They're like sadomasochists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.320
from beyond the grave. In fact, that's what&nbsp;
I originally wanted to call the picture."

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.200
As far as titles go, it's certainly a bit beyond&nbsp;
the pale, and definitely a lot different from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.800
"The Hellbound Heart," the title of the&nbsp;
novella that inspired the film. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:06.000
if there was ever to be a gang name that nailed&nbsp;
the vibe that Pinhead, Butterball, and the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.720
of the cenobites give off, "Sadomasochists&nbsp;
From Beyond the Grave" is perfectly spot on.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.520
Alongside Leatherface, Michael Myers,&nbsp;
Freddy Krueger, and Pennywise, Pinhead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:20.640
is an almost universally recognizable villain&nbsp;
and an iconic embodiment of '80s horror.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:24.960
It might be surprising, then, that in the first&nbsp;
"Hellraiser" films, he's not technically a bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.160
guy — just an infernal ambassador who takes his&nbsp;
job extremely seriously. According to his creator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.440
though, the moniker "Pinhead" doesn't&nbsp;
exactly do the character justice.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.360
Clive Barker explained in an interview with&nbsp;
Dread Central that the character was a priest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.400
of hell who remained nameless&nbsp;
in the original script. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:45.360
a special effects artist referred to him as&nbsp;
"Pinhead," and that was that. Barker recalled —

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.480
"I was like, 'Wait a second, you&nbsp;
just named my villain Pinhead?'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.120
I didn't think that sounded very dignified. But&nbsp;
it ended up on the call sheet, and it just stuck."

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.120
"Who you callin' 'Pinhead?'"

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.560
Before the release of "Hellraiser,"&nbsp;
no one realized how popular the guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.400
with nails in his face would end up being.&nbsp;
The hell priest wasn't even the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.640
poster boy for the film: Pinhead actor&nbsp;
Doug Bradley told HorrorOnline.com that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:11.360
a flayed Frank was originally the image&nbsp;
intended to grace posters and lunchboxes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.000
At some point during production, the powers&nbsp;
that be opted for the grid-faced man to push&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.160
the film into the darkest corners of the&nbsp;
public consciousness. Bradley explained —

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.120
"The image is so original, so startling.&nbsp;
It is just an incredible image to look at,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.720
and that made a big difference in terms&nbsp;
of the public's perception of the movie."

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.840
On one level, "Hellraiser" is a cautionary tale&nbsp;
about what happens to a curious individual when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.600
he meddles in the supernatural. In another&nbsp;
sense, if you look past the flesh flaying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.360
the screams, the carnage, and the sadist&nbsp;
with pins in his face, you'll find what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.240
Clive Barker has referred to as "a love story&nbsp;
from beyond the grave." It might sound absurd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.560
but take a closer look and you'll see that&nbsp;
Barker isn't wrong. The author explained that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.880
just as it's desire that leads Frank Cotton&nbsp;
to a lonely place where he's skinned alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.960
it's the same desire that leads his brother's&nbsp;
wife, Julia Cotton, down the road to ruin.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.880
Julia attempts to resurrect her old&nbsp;
lover by butchering unsuspecting men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:06.160
so Frank can feed on their precious&nbsp;
blood, but as Barker points out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.320
she doesn't do it because she's a psychopath&nbsp;
— she does it for love. Barker stressed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:15.040
the same interview that he intended "Hellraiser"&nbsp;
to be an "intelligent and popular horror movie"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:18.960
that didn't exploit women or manipulate&nbsp;
sensibilities. He told Monsterland —

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:23.440
"There has been a general move towards&nbsp;
the infantile in horror pictures recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:27.360
a desire to soften the blows. I hope this&nbsp;
time we're going to get tough again."

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.640
Let's be honest — the Cenobites are a pretty&nbsp;
eye-catching bunch. If Pinhead and his posse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.440
strutted into a party, it would certainly cause&nbsp;
a stir. Their look is hellish, and with all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.440
leather and boots it's reminiscent of the&nbsp;
weirdest shock rock group never to play a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:46.000
gig. You wouldn't want to bump into these guys in&nbsp;
a graveyard or an S&amp;M club, the latter of which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.720
according to Clive Barker, is the inspiration for&nbsp;
the Cenobites' distinctive look. Barker told The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.360
Guardian that both the physical and emotional&nbsp;
make-up of the Cenobites was inspired by S&amp;M&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:00.160
clubs he had frequented, specifically an&nbsp;
underground club in New York called Cellblock 28.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:05.040
Barker revealed that Cellblock 28 had a hardcore&nbsp;
S&amp;M night where there was no drinking or drugs:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:09.280
just intense pain, pleasure, and a&nbsp;
whole lot of piercings. He elaborated —

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.160
"It was the first time I ever saw people pierced&nbsp;
for fun. It was the first time I saw blood spilt."

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:20.160
"Oh, no tears, please! It's&nbsp;
a waste of good suffering."

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.840
Barker explained that the move to make&nbsp;
the cenobites act and dress a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:27.360
way was met with positive reception&nbsp;
from the S&amp;M community, saying —

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.280
"I was validating a lifestyle. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.320
a celebration of the beauty of&nbsp;
these strange secret rituals."

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:37.120
Having only made a couple of short films with&nbsp;
a group of friends in Liverpool as a teenager,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:41.360
"Hellraiser" was Clive Barker's first&nbsp;
experience helming a major movie production.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:45.920
Directing a feature film for the first time was&nbsp;
a daunting task, as he revealed in an interview:

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:50.160
"I didn't know the rules. I didn't know who&nbsp;
the sound guy was. I only knew who the camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:55.040
operator was because he was the guy behind the&nbsp;
camera. I don't think that finally hurt the movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:58.320
May have actually been useful because&nbsp;
I sort of made it up as I went along."

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:03.920
When Barker was young, he and a group of friends&nbsp;
grabbed an 8mm camera and shot two shorts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:07.760
"Salome" and "The Forbidden," inspired&nbsp;
by underground and experimental directors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:11.360
like Kenneth Anger. Future Hellraiser&nbsp;
collaborators Doug Bradley and Pete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:15.040
Atkins were involved in the productions.&nbsp;
Barker reflected on his work by saying —

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:18.560
"They are technically extremely&nbsp;
crude and their storylines obscure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:20.960
Actually I think it has a&nbsp;
strange poetry about it."

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.760
That "strange poetry," as&nbsp;
well as the themes of pacts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:28.320
rituals, killers, and sexuality, have a lot in&nbsp;
common with "Hellraiser" as we know it today.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:32.800
The puzzle box in "Hellraiser" with which&nbsp;
Frank opens the doors to another dimension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.440
is integral to the franchise's overarching&nbsp;
plot. The peculiar-looking device — also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.640
known as the Lament Configuration&nbsp;
— was described in "The Hellbound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:45.040
Heart' as one of several in existence that&nbsp;
was created by Frenchman Philip LeMarchand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.360
One is said to be safely under lock and key in&nbsp;
the basement of the Vatican, another was rumored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:53.280
to have been in the possession of the Marquis&nbsp;
De Sade during his tenure at the Bastille,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:56.720
and a third found its way into the&nbsp;
hands of an Englishman named Frank.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:59.760
"Let me see—"
"No, don't touch it! It's dangerous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:01.680
It opens doors."

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:07.200
The inspiration for the puzzle box came from&nbsp;
an unlikely source: Clive Barker's grandfather.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:10.800
According to an interview with the&nbsp;
podcast The Geek's Guide To The Galaxy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:15.680
Barker wanted his characters to access hell&nbsp;
in a unique way. He was eventually drawn to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:20.160
childhood memory of an elaborate wooden puzzle&nbsp;
box his grandfather, who was a ship's cook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:24.880
brought back from the Far East. Barker recalled&nbsp;
playing with the box a lot as a child. When he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:28.880
was puzzling about how best his character in "The&nbsp;
Hellbound Heart" could open the doors of Hell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:32.240
he remembered his grandfather's box,&nbsp;
and the final piece fell into place.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:36.880
It's no good sweating buckets to create&nbsp;
a horror film designed to shock and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:39.920
unnerve the audience if you pay&nbsp;
little attention to the soundtrack:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:45.840
music adds ambience, atmosphere, suspense, and&nbsp;
dread to a movie. In short, the cenobites can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.560
exactly share screen time with a Disney-style&nbsp;
soundtrack — it would be terribly unbecoming.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:54.080
Before "Hellraiser's" release,&nbsp;
industrial band Coil delivered a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:58.400
sonically charged hellscape of a soundtrack&nbsp;
to accompany Clive Barker's film. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:02.800
it never made the final cut and was replaced&nbsp;
by Christopher Young's more traditional score.

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:04.720
Barker was friendly with the members of Coil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:09.200
and felt their unique sound would make a perfect&nbsp;
fit for "Hellraiser." The studio disagreed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:13.120
apparently deciding that the soundtrack was&nbsp;
just too unsettling. Anyone familiar with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:17.360
Coil's work during that period wouldn't have&nbsp;
been surprised — as Barker himself once said —

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:19.600
"Coil is the only group I've heard on disc,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:23.280
whose records I've taken off&nbsp;
because they make my bowels churn."

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:27.840
However, the truth as to why Coil's "Hellraiser"&nbsp;
soundtrack was never given the green light is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:32.720
far more pedestrian. Coil's Stephen Thrower&nbsp;
told The Quietus that when Barker asked the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:36.560
producers for more special effects funding,&nbsp;
they said something along the lines of,

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:39.840
"We didn't think this was going to be&nbsp;
as commercial as it is. Re-shoot those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:44.160
effects scenes and here's some more money. One&nbsp;
stipulation, we want our man to do the score."

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:49.680
Movie sets are hectic places. Everyone's got a job&nbsp;
to do and everyone's rushing around frantically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:54.640
trying not to get in one another's way. Costume&nbsp;
designers, camera operators, production managers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:58.800
and boom operators all play their part in a&nbsp;
constant effort to keep things from going off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:03.120
the rails. On the set of "Hellraiser," they&nbsp;
also had their very own maggot and cockroach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:07.840
wrangler to keep things in order. It was an&nbsp;
odd job, but a necessary one in a pre-CGI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:11.680
age where everyone had to get their hands&nbsp;
dirty to create something authentic.

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:16.480
In an interview with Film Threat, Clive Barker&nbsp;
pointed out that being on a movie set for fourteen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:20.720
straight hours and dealing with thirty different&nbsp;
people, all with equally important jobs to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:26.080
was far more tiring than he had anticipated. But&nbsp;
relief came with the insects: Barker enthused —

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:29.200
"The moments which were really exciting&nbsp;
were the major cockroach scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:32.320
which I really liked. We had a&nbsp;
roach-wrangler. We had Tupperware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:37.120
you know? The ultimate Tupperware container —&nbsp;
your Roach Tupperware Container — in the fridge."

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:40.800
Barker explained that special passports&nbsp;
and visas were needed to import extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:45.360
large American cockroaches to the UK. The&nbsp;
crew could only import male cockroaches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:49.360
because there was a danger that they&nbsp;
could escape and procreate. Barker joked —

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:52.240
"Somewhere in customs there's&nbsp;
presumably a roach sexer!"

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:57.120
Although many people praised "Hellraiser" upon&nbsp;
its release for breathing new life into the horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:02.400
genre, not everyone was won over by Clive Barker's&nbsp;
vision. Famed film critic Roger Ebert gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:08.320
"Hellraiser" a paltry half-star review, decrying&nbsp;
it as a dreary sham. He wrote, somewhat viciously,

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:11.600
"This is one of those movies you&nbsp;
sit through with mounting dread,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:15.360
as the fear grows inside of you that&nbsp;
it will turn out to be feature-length."

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:19.120
He didn't care much for its sequel,&nbsp;
either, which he made clear on his TV show.

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:22.320
"The problem here is that there is&nbsp;
no discernable beginning, middle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:26.480
or end to this nightmare, just a series&nbsp;
of disconnected and gruesome scenes."

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:30.480
If that seems a tad harsh, it gets worse&nbsp;
— Ebert questioned the motives of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:33.600
audience for going to see the movie&nbsp;
in the first place. In his opinion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:38.400
"Hellraiser" contained no surprises or emotional&nbsp;
engagement, and lacked any wit or flair.

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:42.320
Interestingly, Ebert's harsh verdict on the&nbsp;
film appeared to be the opening shot in a war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:46.960
of words between the legendary critic and Clive&nbsp;
Barker, as chronicled on Ebert's own website.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:50.720
Years after the release of "Hellraiser,"&nbsp;
Barker would slam the critic for making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:54.400
the controversial statement that "Video&nbsp;
games can never be art." Barker described&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:59.040
Ebert as "prejudiced," calling him a, quote,&nbsp;
"pompous, arrogant old man." How much bearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:03.280
Ebert's notorious review of "Hellraiser" had&nbsp;
on Barker's scathing words we'll never know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:08.080
but just as criticism is always subjective,&nbsp;
an artist never forgets a bad review.

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:13.040
Imagine Pinhead and Michael Myers battling it&nbsp;
out in a cinematic crossover along the lines of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:18.240
"Freddy vs. Jason" or "Alien vs. Predator…"&nbsp;
and then imagine it some more, because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:22.480
never going to happen. However, it nearly did.&nbsp;
Although it probably wouldn't have contained a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:27.040
lot of small talk between the chief cenobite and&nbsp;
the masked murderer, "Hellraiser vs. Halloween"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:31.680
was a promising premise nevertheless. Paul Kane's&nbsp;
book, "The Hellraiser Films and Their Legacy,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:36.800
reveals that filmmaker Dave Parker once pitched&nbsp;
the idea of a crossover event to Dimension Films.

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:39.680
Parker needed a way to get the two&nbsp;
villains together for a rumble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:44.160
so he hatched an origin story in which Myers&nbsp;
is trick-or-treating as a child and receives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:48.880
a mysterious box from a stranger in black boots.&nbsp;
Upon opening it, the Lord of the Dead possesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:52.880
Myers' body and goes on a killing spree.&nbsp;
Since he's possessed by a demonic entity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:57.760
Myers cannot be killed until Pinhead shows&nbsp;
up years later. From there, it's game on.

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:01.760
Dimension was excited enough to reach out&nbsp;
to both Clive Barker and John Carpenter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:07.040
and penciled in a release date of October&nbsp;
31, 2004. However, not only did the owners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:11.200
of the "Halloween" movie rights throw shade on&nbsp;
the idea, a poll on the official "Halloween"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:16.080
website suggested that the majority of fans&nbsp;
didn't want the movie to happen… so it didn't.

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:20.000
One of the most unsettling things&nbsp;
about the cenobites — apart from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:24.160
their insatiable desire to inflict&nbsp;
pain — is their unnerving silence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:28.800
Unwavering eye contact with unreadable yet&nbsp;
intense facial expressions, combined with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:33.200
art of keeping your entire body extremely&nbsp;
still, is the embodiment of psychological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:37.680
intimidation. The cenobites are obvious&nbsp;
masters at it. With the exception of Pinhead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:42.000
who's not exactly mister talkative, the rest of&nbsp;
the cenobites in "Hellraiser" are a pretty mute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:46.960
bunch — even the Chatterer isn't much of a&nbsp;
chatterbox. In total, Pinhead is only in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:51.040
original film for eight minutes and doesn't have&nbsp;
a lot of lines. That's why it's no wonder that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:56.080
combined with the industrial make-up, Doug Bradley&nbsp;
thought he would be unrecognizable in character.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:59.040
In an interview with The Guardian,&nbsp;
Bradley explained that he was offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:02.240
both the role of Pinhead and&nbsp;
the bit part of a moving man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:05.840
He had reservations about playing&nbsp;
a heavily made-up monster, saying —

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:10.000
"Because 'Hellraiser' was my first movie, I&nbsp;
thought it might be a good idea to be on screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:14.880
as myself. But it was a brief conversation. There&nbsp;
was no doubt in my mind that this unnamed guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:19.920
with the pins in his head had a certain je ne&nbsp;
sais quoi that a mattress-delivery guy didn't."

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:22.720
The first time Bradley saw&nbsp;
himself in the mirror as Pinhead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:25.680
he knew he had made the right&nbsp;
decision. He continued —

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:28.880
"I had a sense of majesty. His threat is implied:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:31.920
Look what I did to myself — now&nbsp;
imagine what I can do to you."

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:45.120
"Trick us again, child, and your suffering&nbsp;
will be legendary… even in hell."

